-/// Implicitly define all of these helper functions to handle only unshared
-/// types; essentially, these act like `is_unshared_*` functions until shared
-/// support is implemented.
+/// These helpers are shared-AWARE for functions and shared-BLIND for the GC
+/// types, and the difference is deliberate.
+///
+/// `shared` on a FUNC type says the function may be referenced from several
+/// threads. It does not change the signature, the calling convention, or the
+/// ABI — a `(shared (func (result i32)))` is called exactly like a
+/// `(func (result i32))`. So the func accessors look straight through the bit;
+/// refusing to do so is what made every shared-everything-threads intrinsic
+/// panic in `unwrap_func` the moment its trampoline was compiled
+/// (enclave: `thread.available_parallelism`, see docs/wasm-parallelism.md).
+///
+/// `shared` on a STRUCT/ARRAY/CONT type is a different claim entirely: those
+/// are GC objects, and sharing changes allocation, write barriers and
+/// collection. None of that is implemented, so those accessors still act like
+/// `is_unshared_*` and their `unwrap_*` still assert — a shared GC type
+/// reaching them is a real "not yet implemented", not a false alarm.
